{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 31, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a powerful nobleman who ruled the County of Tripoli during the twelfth century. His life was filled with political intrigue and military conflict alongside the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He served as a regent for the young king Baldwin IV and later for his successor Baldwin V. This role placed him at the center of feudal disputes within the Crusader states.\n\n### Early Life and Inheritance\nRaymond was born into a prominent family with deep roots in the Levant. His father was Raymond II who died in an assassination plot by the Hashashin sect. The young Raymond inherited the title of count when he was still a child. He spent his youth under the guardianship of his mother Hodierna. She managed the county affairs until he reached adulthood. Raymond eventually traveled to Antioch to secure his position and expand his influence. His early years established him as a key player in regional politics.\n\n### Regency and Political Struggles\nThe death of King Amalric created a power vacuum that Raymond sought to fill. He became the regent for the leper king Baldwin IV despite opposition from other barons. Raymond tried to maintain stability while the kingdom faced threats from Saladin. He favored a policy of diplomacy with Muslim neighbors rather than constant warfare. This approach caused friction with the militant faction led by Guy of Lusignan. Raymond even formed an alliance with Saladin at one point to protect his own interests. These actions made him unpopular with many Latin Christians who viewed him as a traitor.\n\n### Final Years and Death\nRaymond retired to his castle in Tibnin after falling out of favor with the royal court. He died in 1187 shortly before the catastrophic Battle of Hattin. His death removed a major obstacle for Guy of Lusignan and the aggressive war faction. The loss of Raymond contributed to the disunity that allowed Saladin to conquer Jerusalem. His legacy remains complex due to his shifting alliances and controversial political choices. Historians debate whether he was a pragmatic leader or a self-serving opportunist. The County of Tripoli survived for another century after his passing but never regained its former strength. His story illustrates the fragile nature of the Crusader states during this turbulent period.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0049892943}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -2.3841887e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0019425532}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.10188125}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.010168037}, {"token": " powerful", "logprob": -0.7904258}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.0070277657}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.37693268}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -0.028098367}, {"token": " ruled", "logprob": -0.13574056}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.039166257}, {"token": " County", "logprob": -0.00068210973}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -3.5762793e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.0069515365}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.03940635}, {"token": " tw", "logprob": -0.0037144779}, {"token": "elfth", "logprob": -5.960466e-07}, {"token": " century", "logprob": -4.8875927e-06}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.02555354}, {"token": " His", "logprob": -1.5020365}, {"token": " life", "logprob": -0.05703065}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.2842034}, {"token": " filled", "logprob": -1.1167905}, {"token": " with", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " political", "logprob": -0.026916588}, {"token": " intrigue", "logprob": -0.13430573}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.000807671}, {"token": " military", "logprob": -0.0028512557}, {"token": " conflict", "logprob": -0.08162169}, {"token": " alongside", "logprob": -4.9877377}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.14461192}, {"token": " Kingdom", "logprob": -0.10362702}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.0728842e-06}, {"token": " Jerusalem", 